      "blogpost1.title": "How to Compress a PDF for Free Without Losing Quality | Code Engineer",
      "blogpost1.meta": "A step-by-step guide to shrinking a PDF's file size for free in your browser, without ruining the images or text quality — plus what actually makes PDFs so large.",
      "blogpost1.h1": "How to Compress a PDF for Free Without Losing Quality",
      "blogpost1.date": "September 9, 2026",
      "blogpost1.p.intro": "A PDF that's too big to email, too slow to upload to a job application form, or eating up phone storage is one of the most common small annoyances online. The good news: you can usually shrink a PDF by 30-70% without any visible drop in quality, and you don't need to install anything or hand the file over to a random website to do it.",
      "blogpost1.h2.why": "Why PDFs get so big in the first place",
      "blogpost1.p.why": "Text itself takes up almost no space — a 40-page contract of pure text is often under 200 KB. The size almost always comes from embedded images: a scanned document saved as a PDF, screenshots pasted into a report, or photos dropped into a brochure. Scanners in particular tend to save each page as an oversized, barely-compressed image \"just in case,\" which is why a 10-page scanned form can easily balloon to 20-30 MB. Leftover metadata (editing history, thumbnails, unused fonts) adds a smaller but still real amount on top.",
      "blogpost1.h2.steps": "How to compress a PDF in 3 steps (free, no signup)",
      "blogpost1.steps.1": "Open the PDF Compressor and choose your file. It loads directly in your browser tab — nothing is uploaded to a server, so this works fine even for files with sensitive information.",
      "blogpost1.steps.2": "Click Compress. The tool strips unnecessary metadata and re-encodes the embedded images at a slightly lower JPEG quality, which is where most of the size reduction comes from.",
      "blogpost1.steps.3": "Compare the before/after file size shown on screen, then download the smaller file. If a page count or preview looks off, nothing was changed on your device — you can just try again or use the original.",
      "blogpost1.h2.quality": "Will compressing actually hurt the quality?",
      "blogpost1.p.quality": "Used carefully, no — not in any way you'd notice. The compressor only re-saves an embedded image if the result is genuinely smaller, and it skips image types (like CMYK scans or images using unusual color profiles) where re-encoding risks a visible color shift, leaving those untouched rather than guessing. Text stays perfectly sharp either way, since PDF text isn't stored as pixels — only embedded images go through any recompression at all.",
      "blogpost1.h2.tips": "A few more tips if you need it even smaller",
      "blogpost1.tips.1": "If the PDF is a scan, try re-scanning at 150-200 DPI instead of 300+ DPI for anything that isn't fine print — it's the single biggest lever for scanned documents.",
      "blogpost1.tips.2": "Remove pages you don't actually need to send (cover pages, blank pages, appendices the recipient won't read) before compressing.",
      "blogpost1.tips.3": "If you're the one creating the PDF, paste images into the document at the size they'll actually be displayed, not at full camera resolution.",
      "blogpost1.tips.4": "For a document that's mostly text with a few images, compressing usually gets you most of the way there in one pass — there's rarely a need to compress the same file over and over.",
      "blogpost1.p.cta": "Ready to shrink your own file? It takes about ten seconds and nothing leaves your device.",
      "blogpost1.cta.link": "Try the free PDF Compressor →",

